"""Render self-contained HTML and concise Markdown from saved aggregate results."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,base64,html
import pandas as pd
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
r=json.loads((P/'results.json').read_text()); a=r['raw']; b=r['legacy_weighted']; d=r['diagnostics']; c=r['counts']; ch=r['checks']
def ci(x,k,s=1):return f"{x[k]*s:.2f} ({x[k+'_ci'][0]*s:.2f} to {x[k+'_ci'][1]*s:.2f})"
def figure(name,caption):
    img=base64.b64encode((P/f'figures/{name}.png').read_bytes()).decode()
    return f'<figure><img src="data:image/png;base64,{img}" alt="{html.escape(caption)}"><figcaption>{caption}</figcaption></figure>'
def table(headers,rows):return '<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join(f'<th>{v}</th>' for v in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join(f'<td>{v}</td>' for v in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'
lead=f"Day-1 RHC was associated with higher recorded 30-day mortality: {100*a['risk_rhc']:.1f}% versus {100*a['risk_no_rhc']:.1f}%, an observed difference of {100*a['rd']:.2f} percentage points (95% CI {100*a['rd_ci'][0]:.2f} to {100*a['rd_ci'][1]:.2f}). This comparison does not establish that RHC caused the difference."
limitations=[('Critical · timing','RHC is recorded on the first day of SUPPORT eligibility. Day-1 physiology, DNR, APACHE and prognosis scores may precede or follow catheterization; ADL/DASI assessment timing is also insufficiently specified. Dates lack within-day treatment and measurement ordering.','Retrieve the measurement protocol, eligibility time, catheter insertion time and early-event handling; classify each adjustment variable before causal modeling.'),('Critical · identification','Clinical severity and treatment decisions can cause both RHC use and mortality. The legacy exclusion list is not a sufficient adjustment-set argument. Measured balance does not establish exchangeability.','Have clinical and statistical reviewers specify a causal diagram, treatment strategies and a justified pre-exposure adjustment set.'),('High · missing data','Single median imputation and reference-category encoding suppress missing-data uncertainty; cat2 absence could be structural or undocumented disease. MAR and MNAR are not established.','Clarify cat2 semantics and prior preprocessing. After timing adjudication, design outcome-inclusive, type-appropriate multiple imputation under an explicit MAR assumption and clinically grounded MNAR shifts.'),('High · day-1 selection','Exposure uses information accumulated during day 1. Eligibility, treatment assignment and follow-up may be misaligned. No t3d30 values below 2 are present; that alone cannot establish whether early deaths were excluded.','Recover upstream inclusion and early-death rules. Consider a justified grace-period design or a day-1 landmark with its changed survivor population; do not silently remove early events.'),('High · model and overlap','Penalized main-effects logistic propensity scores, limited overlap and weight clipping can leave confounding or change the weighted comparison. Clipping is not trimming and does not repair structural nonpositivity.','Review tails, nonlinearities and interactions, and choose an estimand and overlap policy before a confirmatory reanalysis.'),('High · outcome and transport','Eleven patients coded dth30=No have last-contact dates before day 30. Complete dth30 coding does not itself prove complete mortality ascertainment. Study-center dependence and a critically ill historical cohort limit uncertainty and generalization.','Verify 30-day ascertainment and center information. Do not transport these numbers directly to current ICU practice.')]
sections=[]
sections.append('<section><p class="eyebrow">01 / Population and question</p><h2>A cohort comparison, with a defined denominator</h2><p>The primary estimand is the difference and ratio of recorded 30-day death proportions between patients coded RHC and No RHC in the supplied 5,735-person cohort. Unit: one patient; exposure: swang1; outcome: dth30 = Yes. All supplied patients are retained. No RHC means no day-1 RHC, not necessarily never receiving RHC. No predictive train/test split is needed for this descriptive analysis.</p>'+figure('flow','Figure 1. Cohort flow starts at the supplied file, not the original screened population. Exposure and outcome are complete; all 5,735 patients contribute to every displayed estimate. Death counts are observed, not weighted.')+f'<p>Checks: {ch["duplicate_rows"]} duplicate rows; unique patient IDs; {ch["dth30_date_disagreement"]} disagreements between dth30 and recorded death-date minus admission-date ≤30 days; {ch["negative_death_intervals"]} negative death intervals; {ch["no_death30_followup_below30"]} non-deaths with t3d30 below 30; {ch["last_contact_before_day30_among_no_death30"]} non-deaths with last contact before day 30. Minimum t3d30 is {ch["min_t3d30"]:g} days. These are consistency checks, not source-record verification. Date units are treated as the numeric day counts supplied.</p></section>')
sections.append('<section><p class="eyebrow">02 / Estimates and uncertainty</p><h2>The association remains after legacy weighting</h2>'+table(['Comparison','RHC risk','No-RHC risk','RD, percentage points (95% CI)','RR (95% CI)'],[[label,f'{100*x["risk_rhc"]:.2f}%',f'{100*x["risk_no_rhc"]:.2f}%',ci(x,'rd',100),ci(x,'rr')] for label,x in [('Observed · primary',a),('Legacy weighted · audit only',b)]])+figure('effects','Figure 2. Risk difference is RHC minus No RHC; risk ratio is RHC divided by No RHC. Bars show 95% intervals. Observed intervals use independent-binomial normal RD and log-RR approximations. Weighted intervals are percentile intervals from 80 patient bootstrap resamples, with the full legacy preprocessing and propensity fit repeated.')+'<p>The weighted interval describes sampling variation of this particular algorithm. It does not incorporate a defensible missing-data model, timing uncertainty, unmeasured confounding, center clustering or identification uncertainty. With 80 resamples, percentile endpoints have appreciable Monte Carlo variation. These intervals are exploratory and assume independent patient sampling; no multiplicity correction was applied.</p></section>')
sections.append('<section><p class="eyebrow">03 / Diagnostic explorer</p><h2>Inspect the data behind the estimate</h2><label for="panel">Choose a diagnostic view</label> <select id="panel" onchange="switchPanel(this.value)"><option value="missing">Missingness</option><option value="balance">Balance and overlap</option><option value="sensitivity">Specification sensitivity</option></select><div id="missing" class="panel">'+figure('missingness','Figure 3. Missing percentages use the full denominator in each exposure group: 2,184 RHC and 3,551 No RHC. Only fields with missing values are shown. Missing death dates generally reflect survival and must not be treated as ordinary missing covariates; discharge date is excluded from the propensity model.')+f'<p>cat2 is missing in 4,535/5,735 (79.1%), adld3p in 4,296/5,735 (74.9%), and urin1 in 3,028/5,735 (52.8%). Only {ch["candidate_complete_cases"]:,} patients are complete across all legacy candidate fields; no complete-case analysis was substituted. Missingness patterns cannot establish MCAR/MAR/MNAR. The legacy routine maps missing cat2 to the dropped reference level and median-fills numeric covariates. The data may also contain upstream imputations or sentinel values not described in this dictionary; zero missing cells elsewhere do not establish original measurement completeness.</p></div><div id="balance" class="panel">'+figure('diagnostics','Figure 4. Left: the 15 largest pre-weighting absolute SMDs, with a fixed unweighted pooled standard deviation for both comparisons. The 0.10 line is a screening heuristic, not a validity threshold. Right: group-normalized density of fitted propensity scores before clipping. All encoded terms are available in balance.csv.')+f'<p>{d["candidate_fields"]} candidate fields become {d["encoded_terms"]} encoded terms. Mean absolute SMD: {d["smd_mean_before"]:.3f} → {d["smd_mean_after"]:.3f}; maximum after weighting: {d["smd_max_after"]:.3f}; {d["smd_above_010_after"]} terms remain above 0.10. Propensity range: {d["ps_range"][0]:.4f}–{d["ps_range"][1]:.4f}; {d["clipped_n"]} scores clipped at 0.02/0.98. Maximum stabilized weight: {d["weight_quantiles"]["max"]:.2f}; 99th percentile: {d["weight_quantiles"]["p99"]:.2f}. Kish effective sample sizes: RHC {d["ess"]["rhc"]:.0f}, No RHC {d["ess"]["no_rhc"]:.0f}; these describe weight concentration, not actual patient counts. No fitting warnings were captured: {len(d["fit_warnings"])==0}.</p></div><div id="sensitivity" class="panel">'+figure('sensitivity','Figure 5. Exploratory point estimates on the same 5,735 patients. Each clipping row reuses the original fitted propensity scores; missingness coding and demographics/history rows refit the model. No uncertainty intervals were computed for these alternative specifications. The dotted line is the observed risk difference.')+table(['Specification','RD, percentage points','RR'],[[s['specification'],f'{100*s["rd"]:.2f}',f'{s["rr"]:.3f}'] for s in r['sensitivity']])+f'<p>The restricted model uses age, sex, race, education, income, insurance and the 12 history fields; it omits acute severity and is not a sufficient adjustment set. Explicit missingness coding is not multiple imputation or an MNAR analysis. These comparisons probe algorithm choices, not the magnitude of all biases. The legacy weighted point-RR E-value is {r["evalue_point_only"]:.2f}, using RR* + √[RR*(RR*−1)]. It is a benchmark for unmeasured-confounding strength on the RR scale under the E-value assumptions; it cannot repair timing, selection, misclassification or missingness. No E-value confidence-bound claim is made.</p></div></section>')
sections.append('<section><p class="eyebrow">04 / Critical review</p><h2>What the legacy script gets wrong</h2>'+table(['Legacy choice','Correction / interpretation'],[['Excludes only IDs, dates and outcomes; calls everything else baseline','A column exclusion list cannot establish pre-treatment availability or distinguish confounders, mediators and colliders. The estimator is reproduced solely for audit.'],['Calls 0.02/0.98 clipping “trim to overlap”','All patients remain. Scores are winsorized; weights and resulting target comparison are altered.'],['Calls a logistic treatment coefficient “g-computation-style”','That coefficient is a conditional odds ratio, not a marginal standardized risk contrast. With common mortality, OR is not RR; noncollapsibility also prevents simple comparison. That regression is not rerun here.'],['Reports only mean SMD; changes SD denominator after weighting','Mean imbalance can hide individual failures. This audit uses a fixed SD and saves every encoded term, plus maximum SMD, tails and effective sample size.'],['Median imputation with no uncertainty intervals','The reproduction now has a full-pipeline bootstrap, but single imputation remains scientifically unresolved; the interval is not an MI-pooled interval.'],['Describes causal validity as exchangeability plus positivity','Consistency, well-defined interventions, time alignment, appropriate measurement/selection, missing-data assumptions and model adequacy also matter. An E-value is not a readiness certificate.']])+'</section>')
sections.append('<section><p class="eyebrow">05 / Identification and next decisions</p><h2>Why a causal interpretation is not ready</h2><div class="callout"><strong>Causal readiness: not_ready.</strong> Descriptive calculation is complete. The legacy causal claim is not scientifically approved. This is a qualified analysis audit, not a finalized causal report or a clinical recommendation.</div><p>A hypothetical target trial would enroll eligible critically ill patients at the first SUPPORT-qualifying time, assign a specified strategy of RHC during day 1 versus no RHC during day 1, and compare 30-day mortality from that same time. The desired contrast could be the marginal ATE on risk-difference and risk-ratio scales in eligible patients. A day-1 grace period, subsequent treatment/crossover, intervention versions and early deaths need explicit rules; the supplied extract cannot resolve them.</p><p><strong>Working causal structure (hypothesis, not verified):</strong> pre-treatment severity and chronic illness → RHC and mortality; unmeasured clinical judgment → RHC and mortality; RHC → later physiology → mortality. Conditioning on physiology after RHC could block mediation or open collider paths. No sufficient adjustment set is certified here, and no biological direction or bias magnitude is inferred from this sketch.</p>'+table(['Severity / issue','Why it matters','Action before causal follow-up'],limitations)+'<p>These priorities arise from same-context method evaluation. Per the study instructions, no work was delegated; independent artifact review is still pending. No human scientific approval has been given. Multiple imputation, a clinically justified MNAR analysis, negative controls and a valid time-aligned causal estimator are omitted because the required scientific definitions are unresolved, not because the checks passed.</p></section>')
sections.append('<section><p class="eyebrow">06 / Reproduce and inspect</p><h2>Local, inspectable and offline</h2><p>Run from this folder:</p><pre>/tmp/biostat-rhc-analysis-env/bin/python analysis.py\n/tmp/biostat-rhc-analysis-env/bin/python build_report.py</pre><p>The calculation uses only input/rhc.csv. No data are fetched at execution time; no external APIs or CDNs are used. The supplied CSV and dictionary are retained unchanged. Numeric ranges are recorded; values were not removed or winsorized without domain adjudication. Full candidate-field summaries, with observed-value denominators and missing counts, are in table1.csv. No baseline p-values are used.</p><details><summary>Full descriptive Table 1 · all legacy candidate fields</summary>'+pd.read_csv(P/'table1.csv').to_html(index=False,border=0)+'</details><details><summary>Reproducibility metadata and source provenance</summary><p>Seed: 20260924. Bootstrap: 80/80 completed. Penalized logistic propensity model: C=1, max_iter=5000, standardized one-hot predictors. Stabilized weights use observed exposure prevalence; within-group normalized weighted means yield risks. Numeric median filling, encoding and scaling are refit in each resample. No outcome-regression OR was reproduced.</p><pre>'+html.escape(json.dumps(r['versions'],indent=2))+'</pre><p>Input SHA256: <code>'+r['source_sha256']+'</code></p><p>Provenance supplied in input/SOURCE.md: Vanderbilt public SUPPORT/RHC archive, <a href="https://hbiostat.org/data/repo/rhc.csv">CSV source</a> and <a href="https://hbiostat.org/data/repo/rhc.html">dictionary source</a>. Original study as identified by the local dictionary: Connors et al. (1996), <em>The effectiveness of RHC in the initial care of critically ill patients</em>, JAMA 276:889–897; supplied PMID 8782638. The original article and original matching analysis were not independently re-reviewed or reproduced. This audit does not assert agreement with its estimates.</p></details><p>Additional artifacts: results.json, balance.csv, missingness.csv, variable_audit.csv, table1.csv, figures/, decision_log.md. Reporting follows relevant STROBE elements; this is not a claim of a complete STROBE-compliant manuscript.</p></section>')
css='''*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#edf2f5;color:#15324f;font:17px/1.65 system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,sans-serif}main{max-width:1140px;margin:auto;padding:40px 26px}header{background:#15324f;color:white;padding:54px;border-radius:18px;margin-bottom:28px}h1{font-size:clamp(32px,5vw,56px);line-height:1.08;letter-spacing:-1.5px;margin:12px 0 25px}h2{font-size:29px;line-height:1.2;margin:6px 0 20px}.eyebrow{font-size:12px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:2px;font-weight:750;color:#007c83}header .eyebrow{color:#9cdee0}.lede{font-size:20px;max-width:870px}.badge{display:inline-block;padding:5px 14px;border:1px solid #9cdee0;border-radius:20px;font-size:13px}section{background:white;border:1px solid #d8e3e9;border-radius:15px;padding:34px;margin-bottom:25px}figure{margin:26px 0}img{width:100%;height:auto}figcaption{font-size:14px;color:#44566b;border-left:3px solid #007c83;padding-left:14px;margin-top:12px}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:14px}th{text-align:left;background:#eaf2f5;color:#15324f}td,th{padding:13px;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid #dce5eb}td:first-child{font-weight:600}.table-wrap{overflow-x:auto}pre{background:#edf3f6;padding:18px;overflow:auto;font-size:13px}code{overflow-wrap:anywhere}select{padding:10px;border:2px solid #007c83;border-radius:6px;font-size:16px;background:white;color:#15324f}a{color:#006c78}.callout{padding:20px;border-left:5px solid #b95625;background:#fff3ec}details{margin:20px 0;overflow:auto}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:650;padding:10px}footer{font-size:13px;text-align:center;color:#526577}@media(max-width:650px){main{padding:12px}header,section{padding:24px}td,th{padding:8px}}@media print{body{background:white}section{break-inside:avoid}.panel{display:block!important}select,label{display:none}header{color:#15324f;background:white}}'''
script="""function switchPanel(id){document.querySelectorAll('.panel').forEach(p=>p.style.display=p.id===id?'block':'none')}switchPanel('missing');"""
(P/'report.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>RHC | Association and its limits</title><style>'+css+'</style></head><body><main><header><p class="eyebrow">SUPPORT / RHC · Observational study audit</p><h1>Higher mortality.<br>Unresolved causality.</h1><span class="badge">5,735 patients · 30-day outcome · local analysis</span><p class="lede">'+lead+'</p></header>'+''.join(sections)+'<footer>Bounded analysis pilot · 24 September 2026 · No human scientific approval</footer></main><script>'+script+'</script></body></html>')
md=f'''# RHC: association and limits

{lead}

- RHC: {c['deaths_rhc']}/{c['rhc']} deaths; No RHC: {c['deaths_no_rhc']}/{c['no_rhc']} deaths. No exclusions; all 5,735 supplied patients retained. Upstream eligibility flow is unavailable.
- Observed RR: **{ci(a,'rr')}**; RD: **{ci(a,'rd',100)} percentage points**, with 95% independent-binomial approximate confidence intervals.
- Legacy weighted risks: {100*b['risk_rhc']:.2f}% vs {100*b['risk_no_rhc']:.2f}%; RD **{ci(b,'rd',100)} percentage points**; RR **{ci(b,'rr')}**. The 95% percentile intervals use 80 patient bootstrap replicates, refitting the legacy pipeline. They do not resolve missing-data, timing or identification uncertainty.
- {d['clipped_n']} scores clipped at 0.02/0.98; no patients trimmed. Effective sample sizes: RHC {d['ess']['rhc']:.0f}, No RHC {d['ess']['no_rhc']:.0f}. Maximum weighted absolute SMD {d['smd_max_after']:.3f}; {d['smd_above_010_after']} encoded terms above 0.10.

## What prevents a causal interpretation

Eleven patients coded as 30-day survivors have last-contact dates before day 30, so outcome ascertainment needs clarification. Day-1 exposure and covariates have unresolved within-day ordering. Eligibility, early deaths and follow-up must be aligned before specifying a target trial; the absence of t3d30 below 2 does not resolve survivor selection. Acute physiology, DNR and prognosis measurements may be downstream of RHC. Clinical indication and judgment can confound the association. No sufficient adjustment set is established.

cat2 (79.1%), ADL (74.9%) and urine output (52.8%) have substantial missingness. Legacy reference-level encoding and median imputation are not valid substitutes for an explicit missingness model. Bootstrap uncertainty is conditional on that procedure, not MI-pooled inference. Missing-category/indicator and clipping sensitivities are exploratory point estimates; no MAR or MNAR mechanism is confirmed. Cat2 may be structurally absent, requiring source clarification.

The legacy logistic treatment coefficient is a conditional OR, not g-computed marginal risk; it is not reproduced. Mean SMD alone hides imbalance. Clipping is not trimming. The point E-value ({r['evalue_point_only']:.2f}) cannot repair timing, selection or missingness. Causal validity also requires consistency, adequate measurement, appropriate models and positivity—not exchangeability alone. All results concern this historical supplied cohort.

**Causal readiness: not_ready.** This is a completed descriptive audit with a qualified computational reproduction, not a completed causal analysis. Independent review is pending; no human scientific approval has been given. Next: verify timing/ascertainment and cat2 meaning, define a clinical DAG and target trial, then develop justified MI and MNAR analyses. No work was delegated.

## Artifacts and reproduction

Open [report.html](report.html) for the self-contained report, embedded figures, interactive diagnostic selector, detailed critique and Table 1. Sources remain local in input/; supplied source attribution identifies Connors et al., JAMA 1996, 276:889–897 (PMID 8782638). The original article was not independently verified here.

```sh
/tmp/biostat-rhc-analysis-env/bin/python analysis.py
/tmp/biostat-rhc-analysis-env/bin/python build_report.py
```

Machine results: [results.json](results.json). Supporting files: balance.csv, missingness.csv, variable_audit.csv, table1.csv, figures/ and decision_log.md. Input SHA256: `{r['source_sha256']}`. Software versions and seed are in results.json. No network, package installation, external API or remote CDN is required.
'''
(P/'report.md').write_text(md)
(P/'decision_log.md').write_text('''# Decision log — 2026-09-24

1. Followed original specialist skills under /Users/amiee/.codex/biostat-superpowers/skills: biostatistics, preprocessing, causal inference, missing data, statistical analysis, method evaluation and reporting. User scope overrides causal-finalization expectations: publish a qualified audit, not a causal claim. No workflow/MCP, prior comparison outputs or other study arms accessed; no delegation.
2. Primary contrast selected before fitting: unadjusted recorded 30-day RD/RR in all supplied patients. Day-1 treatment is first SUPPORT eligibility day per local dictionary. No assertion that all covariates are baseline.
3. Reproduce only legacy propensity/weight estimator with local unchanged CSV. Keep all 53 candidate fields only for this computational audit. No approved sufficient adjustment set exists. Original outcome-regression OR omitted and critiqued.
4. Use 80 seeded patient bootstrap replicates (below 200 cap), refitting preprocessing and propensity scores. Conditional algorithm uncertainty is explicitly distinguished from missing-data and identification uncertainty. No new primary single-imputation analysis claimed.
5. Exploratory same-cohort sensitivities: clipping thresholds, explicit missingness coding, demographics/history subset. No intervals for these alternatives; no clinically calibrated MNAR analysis or confirmation claimed.
6. Fixed pre-weighting pooled SD for all balance comparisons; inspect individual imbalances, overlap, weights and Kish ESS. Retain observed values; save numeric ranges rather than making unsupported outlier exclusions.
7. Self-review identifies critical timing and identification barriers; causal readiness not_ready. Descriptive artifact can be delivered with explicit scope. Human approval and independent review remain absent.
8. Execution note: initial plotting import stalled on operating-system font discovery; final code uses bundled fonts and a study-local cache. An initial 120-resample computation stopped on pandas string-dtype handling before saving results. The corrected final run used 80 resamples (200 total computed across attempts); only the final 80 support the reported interval. No additional bootstrap reruns were performed. All five PNGs were displayed locally and visually inspected.
9. Follow-up priorities: recover early-event/eligibility rules and timestamps; adjudicate day-1 physiology and ADL/DASI timing; clarify cat2 missingness and upstream preprocessing; verify outcome ascertainment; establish DAG and intervention strategy; then plan MI/MNAR and dependence-aware inference.
''')
print('Report rendered:',len((P/'report.html').read_bytes()),'bytes')
